<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- The speaker side-car, always on (speaker identification has to work
     all the time). speakers/run.sh
     names who said each heard line, into speakers_YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl beside the heard log.
     It follows the ears keeper's capture from device to device (SPEAKER_DEVICE=follow).
     A TEMPLATE: render it, then install (no machine paths live here):
       mkdir -p "$(.venv/bin/python -m ears.home log-dir)"   # the voice log folder
       SPEAKER_PY=/path/to/a/python/with/mlx-audio AGENT_REFS=ada \
           bin/render-plist launchd/dev.sessionkit.voice.ears-speakers.plist.in > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.sessionkit.voice.ears-speakers.plist
     bin/render-plist fills @HOME@ and @EARS_REPO@ (the checkout it runs from) itself;
     @SPEAKER_PY@ and @AGENT_REFS@ have no default.
       launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.sessionkit.voice.ears-speakers.plist
     Needs: refs/NAME.wav in @EARS_REPO@/speakers (README), and @SPEAKER_PY@ with mlx-audio
     (the git build: PyPI 0.5.5 has no nemotron_diarization) and the model already cached:
     HF_HUB_OFFLINE is set, so a start never downloads.
     Restarts: a crash or a refusal restarts it (ThrottleInterval apart; a refused reference
     reloads the model each time, so read the log after a load); touch
     @EARS_REPO@/speakers/STOP and it exits 0 and STAYS down until the next load or login.
     The name: run.sh runs a copy of @SPEAKER_PY@'s real binary, bin/ears-speakers in its
     venv, so ps -c and Activity Monitor say ears-speakers, not Python (README).
     The microphone: macOS asks for a grant per binary, so that copy (the venv's
     bin/ears-speakers) under launchd may need its own, again after a python upgrade
     refreshes it. Without one the stream gives only zeros, and every row says
     speaker "unheard", why "no signal". Check ears-speakers.log in the voice log folder
     (run.sh runs in the foreground here; speakers.log is only for run.sh detached) for
     "mic: open" and for the rows.
     It opens the mic only while the keeper's capture runs. Never run it beside a
     hand-started side-car on the same refs: each line would be labelled twice.
     It replaces the interim follow-capture job some Macs ran under an older hand label
     (speakers/README.md, "Replacing follow-capture", names it): boot that out and remove
     its plist in the SAME step that bootstraps this one, so two side-cars never run.
     The SAME label `ears services install` (only speakers) loads its own job under, at the
     same path. Install tells the two apart by CONTENT: the plist it writes carries
     SESSIONKIT_INSTALLED_BY in EnvironmentVariables, and a render of this template does
     not. So install leaves a render here alone and skips its own (`ears services
     status` names it). The reverse is yours: rendering this over the plist install
     wrote REPLACES install's job (bin/render-plist writes only to stdout or -o; the
     redirect is what overwrites).
     Remove:  launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/dev.sessionkit.voice.ears-speakers -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>dev.sessionkit.voice.ears-speakers</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>@EARS_REPO@/speakers/run.sh</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>@EARS_REPO@/speakers</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>HOME</key><string>@HOME@</string>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>SPEAKER_PY</key><string>@SPEAKER_PY@</string>
    <key>SPEAKER_DEVICE</key><string>follow</string>
    <key>SPEAKER_AGENT_REFS</key><string>@AGENT_REFS@</string>
    <key>HF_HUB_OFFLINE</key><string>1</string>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key><false/>
  </dict>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>30</integer>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>@EARS_LOG_DIR@/ears-speakers.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>@EARS_LOG_DIR@/ears-speakers.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
